@page "/projects/synergy"
@using website.Data

<ProjectPage Url="/projects/synergy" Kicker="Personal Project">
                <p class="max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                    Synergy reads a codebase and builds a graph of it: every module, class and function, and how they call, import and inherit from each other.
                    You can then ask that graph questions, in a web dashboard or through an AI coding agent. It runs entirely on your own machine.
                </p>

                <ProjectSection Title="What it does" Icon="fas fa-diagram-project">
                    <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300">
                        <li>• Parses Python, C#, Java, Go, C++, Rust, JavaScript and TypeScript into one typed graph.</li>
                        <li>• Only re-parses files that changed. An incremental run gives exactly the same graph as a full one, and the tests check that.</li>
                        <li>• Saves a snapshot every time you analyse, or of any git commit or tag, and shows what changed between two snapshots, with the changes that affect the most code first.</li>
                        <li>• Draws call-flow diagrams from any entry point.</li>
                        <li>• Runs your tests under a tracer and compares the calls it saw with the graph, which catches dynamic calls that static analysis can't see.</li>
                        <li>• Analyses a released PyPI package straight from its wheel, without installing it.</li>
                    </ul>
                </ProjectSection>

                <ProjectSection Title="Using it with AI coding agents" Icon="fas fa-robot">
                    <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        Coding agents usually find their way around a codebase by searching text. Synergy gives them an MCP server with 21 tools to ask the graph directly:
                        who calls this, what breaks if I change it, how do these two parts connect, what code is never used.
                    </p>
                    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        One command, <code class="rounded bg-white/[.06] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-cy">synergy claude install</code>, sets it up for Claude Code: the server, a skill,
                        and hooks that remind the agent the graph exists when it starts searching with grep. Other agents get the same guidance in an AGENTS.md file.
                        Uninstalling puts every file back exactly as it was.
                    </p>
                </ProjectSection>

                <ProjectSection Title="How it works" Icon="fas fa-gears">
                    <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        Files are read and hashed, then parsed: Python with its own syntax tree, the other languages with tree-sitter. A linking step resolves imports across files,
                        so every reference points at a real definition. The result is stored in SQLite, and three things read it: a command-line tool, a web dashboard, and the MCP server.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mb-5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs text-slate-400">
                        <span class="chip">read</span><span>→</span><span class="chip">parse</span><span>→</span><span class="chip">link</span><span>→</span><span class="chip">SQLite</span><span>→</span><span class="chip">CLI · dashboard · MCP</span>
                    </p>
                    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        The backend is seven Python packages with a strict dependency order, from the core data model up to the API and the MCP server. The dashboard is a React and TypeScript app
                        with a graph explorer, history diffs, UML diagrams and a view of exactly what an agent is shown.
                    </p>
                </ProjectSection>

